WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.872 --> 00:00:15.681
Anne and were sitting under an apple tree in the orchard behind Patty's Place.

2
00:00:16.702 --> 00:00:18.443
Anne looked as if she were going to cry.

3
00:00:19.384 --> 00:00:22.326
Gilbert's face was downcast, as he waited for her to speak.

4
00:00:23.467 --> 00:00:27.209
"I can't be your wife," said Anne miserably.

5
00:00:27.229 --> 00:00:32.978
"Oh, Gilbert you you've spoiled everything!"

6
00:00:33.938 --> 00:00:35.219
She dared not look at him.

7
00:00:35.239 --> 00:00:39.162
"Don't you care for me at all?"

8
00:00:39.482 --> 00:00:41.363
Gilbert asked after a long pause.

9
00:00:41.383 --> 00:00:44.966
"No, not in that way," Anne said in a low tone.

10
00:00:44.986 --> 00:00:50.770
"I do care a great deal for you as a friend, but I don't love you, Gilbert."

11
00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:54.712
"But can't you give me some hope that you will one day?"

12
00:00:55.152 --> 00:00:55.973
Gilbert asked.

13
00:00:57.334 --> 00:01:00.116
"No, I can't," said Anne desperately.

14
00:01:01.052 --> 00:01:04.275
"I can never, never love you in that way, Gilbert.

15
00:01:04.875 --> 00:01:06.056
I'm so sorry.

16
00:01:06.857 --> 00:01:08.478
Please don't speak about this again."

17
00:01:09.339 --> 00:01:10.740
Gilbert's face was pale.

18
00:01:11.681 --> 00:01:13.122
Anne shuddered and looked away.

19
00:01:14.043 --> 00:01:16.465
There was nothing romantic about this proposal.

20
00:01:17.366 --> 00:01:20.028
Would she ever forget the crushed look on Gilbert's face?

21
00:01:20.048 --> 00:01:22.850
"Is there anybody else?"

22
00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:24.511
Gilbert asked at last.

23
00:01:25.452 --> 00:01:27.194
"No," said Anne quickly.

24
00:01:27.214 --> 00:01:32.874
"I don't care for anyone like that—and I like you better than anybody else in the world.

25
00:01:33.774 --> 00:01:35.495
We must keep on being friends.

26
00:01:36.395 --> 00:01:38.337
I love being friends with you, Gilbert!"

27
00:01:39.297 --> 00:01:41.318
Gilbert gave a small bitter laugh.

28
00:01:42.999 --> 00:01:46.221
"Friends! Your friendship can't satisfy me, Anne.

29
00:01:46.981 --> 00:01:49.583
I want your love," Gilbert said firmly.

30
00:01:49.603 --> 00:01:51.744
"I'm sorry.

31
00:01:52.624 --> 00:01:55.466
Forgive me, Gilbert," was all Anne could say.

32
00:01:55.486 --> 00:01:58.868
"There isn't anything to forgive," he said quietly.

33
00:01:59.672 --> 00:02:02.013
"There have been times when I thought you did love me.

34
00:02:02.893 --> 00:02:04.994
I guess I've deceived myself, that's all.

35
00:02:05.695 --> 00:02:06.375
Good-bye, Anne."

36
00:02:07.195 --> 00:02:11.257
Gilbert walked slowly away, leaving Anne sitting in the orchard alone.

37
00:02:13.278 --> 00:02:17.219
As soon as Gilbert was out of sight, Anne ran inside, to her room, and lay on her

38
00:02:17.239 --> 00:02:18.480
bed, crying bitterly.

39
00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:22.521
She felt as if something very precious had gone out of her life.

40
00:02:23.402 --> 00:02:25.162
It was Gilbert's friendship, of course.

41
00:02:26.143 --> 00:02:28.604
Phil heard Anne's sobs and came in quickly.

42
00:02:29.356 --> 00:02:30.556
"What's the matter, sweetie?"

43
00:02:30.996 --> 00:02:31.557
asked Phil.

44
00:02:32.937 --> 00:02:33.917
Anne did not answer.

45
00:02:34.858 --> 00:02:37.098
She wished Phil were a thousand miles away.

46
00:02:37.118 --> 00:02:40.519
"I suppose you've gone and refused Gilbert Blythe!"

47
00:02:40.959 --> 00:02:41.759
Phil exclaimed.

48
00:02:41.779 --> 00:02:44.260
"You are an idiot, Anne Shirley!"

49
00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:48.501
"Is it idiotic to refuse to marry a man I don't love?"

50
00:02:49.021 --> 00:02:50.042
asked Anne coldly.

51
00:02:50.062 --> 00:02:53.503
"You don't know love when you see it," Phil insisted.

52
00:02:53.523 --> 00:02:58.144
"You think love is a storybook romance with a mysterious handsome stranger!"

53
00:03:00.728 --> 00:03:03.909
"Phil," pleaded Anne, "please leave me alone.

54
00:03:04.829 --> 00:03:06.510
My world has fallen apart.

55
00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:08.491
I want to rebuild it."

56
00:03:09.431 --> 00:03:14.012
"You may have to do that without Gilbert in it now," said Phil, going out the door.

57
00:03:14.032 --> 00:03:16.613
A world without any Gilbert in it!

58
00:03:17.393 --> 00:03:18.494
Anne couldn't consider it.

59
00:03:19.394 --> 00:03:20.734
Well, it was all Gilbert's fault!

60
00:03:21.515 --> 00:03:23.575
He had spoiled their friendship, Anne decided.

61
00:03:24.495 --> 00:03:25.896
She would just learn to live without it.

62
00:03:28.596 --> 00:03:33.077
The next day was a Saturday, and Phil insisted that Anne accompany her on a quick visit to

63
00:03:33.117 --> 00:03:37.179
Bolingbroke, which was Phil's hometown and the town where Anne was born.

64
00:03:38.299 --> 00:03:43.781
Phil knew that Anne was badly shaken by Gilbert's proposal and hoped that the distraction of a weekend

65
00:03:43.841 --> 00:03:45.161
trip would make her feel better.

66
00:03:47.042 --> 00:03:53.023
Anne enjoyed the dances, picnics, and dinners during her stay at Phil's lively family home, except in the

67
00:03:53.063 --> 00:03:54.564
moments when she thought about Gilbert.

68
00:03:56.032 --> 00:03:59.354
The happiest part of the weekend, though, was Anne's visit to her birthplace.

69
00:04:00.135 --> 00:04:05.719
In an out-of-the-way street, she saw the rundown little yellow house that she had so often dreamed about.

70
00:04:06.739 --> 00:04:08.380
Anne's eyes were filled with delight.

71
00:04:09.841 --> 00:04:14.384
She and Phil opened the gate, walked down the little path, and knocked on the front door.

72
00:04:16.085 --> 00:04:18.487
A very tall, thin, old lady answered.

73
00:04:19.368 --> 00:04:25.632
"Yes, the Shirleys lived here twenty years ago," she said in answer to Anne's question.

74
00:04:27.108 --> 00:04:30.829
"They rented the house when I used to live down the street.

75
00:04:31.729 --> 00:04:32.650
I remember them.

76
00:04:33.550 --> 00:04:35.951
They both died of fever at once.

77
00:04:37.031 --> 00:04:42.533
It was terribly sad because they left a baby; I guess it died long ago.

78
00:04:42.553 --> 00:04:44.913
It was a sickly thing."

79
00:04:44.933 --> 00:04:48.594
"It didn't die," said Anne, smiling.

80
00:04:48.614 --> 00:04:50.375
"I was that baby."

81
00:04:50.395 --> 00:04:53.636
"Really? I don't believe it!

82
00:04:54.760 --> 00:05:00.584
Why, you've grown," exclaimed the woman, as if she were surprised that Anne was not still a baby.

83
00:05:00.604 --> 00:05:04.928
"Oh, yes," she continued.

84
00:05:04.948 --> 00:05:07.710
"I see the resemblance now.

85
00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:13.954
Your father had red hair, but your eyes and your nose are like your mother's.

86
00:05:14.875 --> 00:05:17.036
They were both lovely people."

87
00:05:17.056 --> 00:05:20.239
"Could I have a look inside the house?"

88
00:05:20.739 --> 00:05:21.800
asked Anne eagerly.

89
00:05:22.768 --> 00:05:26.732
"Yes, you can look around if you like," the old woman said.

90
00:05:26.752 --> 00:05:29.194
"It won't take long."

91
00:05:30.635 --> 00:05:33.597
Anne went up the narrow stairs and into the little bedroom.

92
00:05:34.478 --> 00:05:38.842
So this was the room where she had been born and where her mother had later died.

93
00:05:39.662 --> 00:05:42.865
Anne looked about her reverently; her eyes filled with tears.

94
00:05:44.126 --> 00:05:47.289
Anne went back downstairs, and the old lady met her in the hall.

95
00:05:47.529 --> 00:05:50.852
She held out a dusty little packet tied with faded blue ribbon.

96
00:05:52.356 --> 00:05:57.799
"Here's a bundle of old letters I found in that closet upstairs when I moved in.

97
00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:04.063
The name on the top letter is Bertha Willis, your mother's maiden name.

98
00:06:05.004 --> 00:06:06.705
I think you should take them."

99
00:06:06.725 --> 00:06:09.686
"Oh, thank you, thank you!"

100
00:06:10.067 --> 00:06:12.168
cried Anne, holding the packet tightly.

101
00:06:12.188 --> 00:06:15.370
"I don't have anything that belonged to my mother.

102
00:06:15.390 --> 00:06:18.572
I I can never thank you enough."

103
00:06:20.196 --> 00:06:24.158
Phil and Anne left the little yellow house, and caught the train back to Halifax.

104
00:06:25.018 --> 00:06:30.600
Throughout the journey Anne fingered the faded ribbon on the packet, but she waited until she was home

105
00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:32.861
at Patty's Place to read the precious letters.

106
00:06:34.101 --> 00:06:38.523
There were not many of them, and some were written by her mother, others by her father.

107
00:06:40.083 --> 00:06:42.164
They both wrote of how much they loved the other.

108
00:06:43.008 --> 00:06:49.335
In the sweetest letter, Anne's mother wrote proudly about her new baby, saying, "I love her best when

109
00:06:49.395 --> 00:06:52.018
she's asleep and even more when she's awake."

110
00:06:52.038 --> 00:06:56.883
"This has been the most beautiful day of my life," Anne thought.

111
00:06:56.903 --> 00:06:59.566
"I've found my mother and father.

112
00:07:00.207 --> 00:07:02.129
Those letters have made them real.

113
00:07:02.850 --> 00:07:04.532
I'm not an orphan anymore."
